{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) as a non-inductive basis for preference, but this fails to address the problem's core challenge: that acting on a theory logically entails an expectation of future success. If the preference does not assume the future will resemble the past (i.e., that a theory with high verisimilitude now will continue to perform well or remain unfalsified), the preference is irrational for action; if it does make this assumption, it smuggles in the very induction Popper rejected. The target asserts the distinction between 'critical preference' and 'probabilistic belief' without demonstrating how this preference avoids the inductive leap required to justify action based on past test results.","premise":"Acting on a theory requires an expectation that it will not fail immediately in the future, which is an inductive assumption about the uniformity of nature or the stability of the theory's performance.","successor_question":"Can you specify a decision rule based on verisimilitude that justifies action without implicitly assuming that a theory which has survived past tests is more likely to survive future tests?","target":"cff2db8ac5144e16e0e753dbec66f66a00ce11007e14da2b3e751af4c4136eae"}]}